\section{Canonical Definitions}

\subsection{Sovereign Engine}

A sovereign engine is defined as:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{E}_{\text{sovereign}}
  =
  \big(
    \Psi,
    \mathcal{R},
    \mathcal{L},
    \mathcal{S}
  \big),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Psi$ is the multi-domain state vector,
  \item $\mathcal{R}$ is the invariant stack,
  \item $\mathcal{L}$ is the layered architecture,
  \item $\mathcal{S}$ is the stress-testing suite.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Invariant}

An invariant is a domain-specific constraint:
\begin{equation}
  R_i : \quad f_i(\Psi) \le \epsilon_i,
\end{equation}
that must remain satisfied for all admissible evolutions of $\Psi$.

\subsection{Anomaly}

An anomaly is any deviation from expected domain behavior:
\begin{equation}
  S_{\text{anomaly}}
  =
  \Psi_{\text{obs}}
  -
  \Psi_{\text{model}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Sovereign Control}

Sovereign control is the mechanism that enforces invariants and suppresses anomalies:
\begin{equation}
  \frac{dS_{\text{anomaly}}}{dt}
  =
  -k_{\text{purge}} S_{\text{anomaly}}
  +
  S_{\text{inject}}.
\end{equation}

